          "title": "You'll want to know: la lata",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la lata — \"the tin, the can; a nuisance\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "En el armario hay latas de atún. In the cupboard there are tins of tuna."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "¡Qué lata! — what a pain! Dar la lata — to pester."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "lata is usually traced to Late Latin latta 'a lath, a thin board', probably of Germanic origin. EtymDB traces its French cousin latte to Frankish latta, the same Germanic word as English lath. The sense moved from a thin strip of wood to a thin sheet of tin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
